8 able to create stand-alone functions is extremely critical in larger projects where one programmer’s code must not accidentally affect another’s code. Another way to structure and compartmentalize code in C is through the use of code blocks. A code block is a logically connected group of program statements that is treated as a unit. In C, you create a code block by placing a sequence of statements between opening and closing curly braces. In this example,

if (x < 10) {
printf("Too low, try again.\n");
scanf("%d", &x);
}

the two statements after the if and between the curly braces are both executed if x is less than 10. These two statements together with the braces represent a code block. They are a logical unit: one of the statements cannot execute without the other executing also. Code blocks allow many algorithms to be implemented with clarity, elegance, and efficiency.

