int int_pwr(register int m, register int e)
{
register int temp;
temp = 1;
for(; e; e--) temp = temp * m;
return temp;
}

In this example, e, m, and temp are declared as register variables because they are all used within the loop. The fact that register variables are optimized for speed makes them ideal for control of or use in loops. Generally, register variables are used where they will do the most good, which are often places where many references will be made to the same variable. This is important because you can declare any number of variables as being of type register, but not all will receive the same access speed optimization. The number of register variables optimized for speed within any one code block is determined by both the environment and the specific implementation of C/C++.

