C++ You might wonder why C++ contains the two virtually equivalent keywords struct and class. This seeming redundancy is justified for several reasons. First, there is no fundamental reason not to increase the capabilities of a structure. In C, structures already provide a means of grouping data. Therefore, it is a small step to allow them to include member functions. Second, because structures and classes are related, it may be easier to port existing C programs to C++. Finally, although struct and class are virtually equivalent today, providing two different keywords allows the definition of a class to be free to evolve.

