As the table shows, a file may be opened in either text or binary mode. In text mode, some character translations may occur. For example, newlines may be converted into carriage return/linefeed sequences. No such translations occur on binary files. The correct method of opening a file is illustrated by this code fragment:

FILE *fp;
if ((fp = fopen("test", "w"))==NULL) {
printf("Cannot open file.\n");
exit(1);
}

This method detects any error in opening a file, such as a write-protected or a full disk, before attempting to write to it. If you use fopen( ) to open a file for output, any preexisting file by that name will be erased and a new file started. If no file by that name exists, one will be created.

