The printf( ) function writes to stdout the arguments that comprise the argument list as specified by the string pointed to by format. The string pointed to by format consists of two types of items. The first type is made up of characters that will be printed on the screen. The second type contains format specifiers that define the way the arguments are displayed. A format specifier begins with a percent sign and is followed by the format code. There must be exactly the same number of arguments as there are format specifiers, and the format specifiers and the arguments are matched in order. For example, the following printf( ) call displays "Hi c 10 there!".

printf("Hi %c %d %s", 'c', 10, "there!");

